<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potsdam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Potsdam is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> just south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. A residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a> onwards, it became a garrison town under Frederick William I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> built the palace of Sanssouci there. Its palaces and parks are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and in 1945 it hosted the Potsdam Conference.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potsdam.jpg" alt="Potsdam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kai Vogel · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Havel-Park-Lake-Babelsberg-Downtown-Potsdam-Green.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Potsdam lies on the River Havel, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, about 25 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s city centre. The area was formed from a series of large moraines left after the last glacial period, and the hilly landscape holds about 20 lakes and rivers in and around the city, among them the Griebnitzsee, Templiner See and Jungfernsee. Only a quarter of the city is built up, the rest remaining green space. The highest point is the Kleiner Ravensberg, at 114 metres. The name of the city and of many of its boroughs is of Slavic origin; it seems originally to have been Poztupimi, perhaps from a West Slavonic term meaning beneath the oaks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area shows signs of occupation since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. After the migrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> moved in, and Potsdam was probably founded after the 7th century as a settlement of the Hevelli tribe centred on a castle. It was first mentioned in 993, when Emperor Otto III gave the territory to Quedlinburg Abbey, and it received its town charter in 1345. In 1573 it was still a small market town of about 2,000 people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> cost it nearly half its population. It had been a Hohenzollern possession since 1415, and in 1660 the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> chose it as a hunting residence.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam">Edict of Potsdam</a> of 1685 offered religious freedom and made the city a centre of European immigration, drawing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> and settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The royal residences were built mainly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, including Sanssouci, by Georg Wenzeslaus von Knobelsdorff, in 1744.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, the court remained in Potsdam, and in 1914 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> signed the Declaration of War in the New Palace. The city lost its status as a second capital in 1918. On 21 March 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> shook hands in Potsdam's Garrison Church in the ceremony known as the Day of Potsdam. Allied bombing raids, notably on the Old Town on 14 April 1945, severely damaged the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Potsdam is the capital and largest city of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>/Brandenburg Metropolitan Region. Its parks and palaces, among them Sanssouci and Cecilienhof Palace, form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest World Heritage Site. Babelsberg in the south-east has a film studio founded in 1912, the oldest large-scale film studio in the world, and Potsdam was named a UNESCO City of Film in 2019. The city became a centre of science in the 19th century, and it has the University of Potsdam and more than 30 research institutes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potsdam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Frederick William I and Frederick the Great the Mark was the heartland of the kingdom, with Berlin and <b>Potsdam</b> as its seats of government and garrisons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After an imperial intervention, Japan told the Allies it would accept the <b>Potsdam</b> terms provided the Emperor's prerogatives were kept.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Glienicke Bridge between West Berlin and <b>Potsdam</b>, the US pilot shot down in 1960 was swapped for the KGB colonel Rudolf Abel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
